// Upgrades. Unlike a reforge, these stack up on an item, up to `max`.
//
// An item with an `upgrade "<id>"` line applies one (see items/upgrades.kdl):
//     /relic upgrade   uses one from your offhand on the main-hand item
//
// name    Shown in the item's lore next to its progress, like "Polish 7/10". It
//         and the filled pips use the rarity color of the item that applies it.
//         When `max` pips don't fit beside the name, it shows only the numbers,
//         so shorter names can have a higher `max` before losing them.
// max     How many one item can take.
// bonus   What each one adds. The first `bonus` that fits an item is the one it
//         gets, so put specific ones above general ones.
//   slots   Only items in these slots (mainhand, offhand, head, chest, legs, feet).
//   kinds   Only items of these `kind`s, ignoring case.
//   stats   What it adds, like in an item. `damage` and `attack-speed` only reach
//           main-hand items that already have that stat.
//
// `element="inherit"` follows the item's own elements for that stat, in the same
// proportions: on `damage 5` + `damage 2 element="fire"`, `damage 7
// element="inherit"` adds 5 neutral and 2 fire. On an item without that stat it
// is untagged. Gems and reforges don't count, so they can't change it later.
//
// Stacks hold only the count, so editing an upgrade changes every item that has
// it. Lowering `max` caps items that are over it; raising it again gives it back.

upgrade "polish" {
    name "Polish"
    max 10

    bonus {
        kinds "Sword"
        stats {
            damage 0.5 element="inherit"
        }
    }
    bonus {
        slots "head" "chest" "legs" "feet"
        stats {
            health 0.5
            defense 1 element="inherit"
        }
    }
}
